<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!-- The line a male wielder calls out when UMW_RallyingCry fires (AbilityDefs/RallyingCry.xml), played
       through the vanilla CompProperties_AbilityEffect.soundMale hook on our rally comp.

       A deliberate self-contained COPY of Core's `Speech_Throne_Male`
       (Data/Core/Defs/SoundDefs/Interact_Sustainers_Work.xml) rather than a reference to it, for one
       hard reason: Core's def is `<sustain>True</sustain>`, and SoundStarter.PlayOneShot hard
       Log.Errors on a sustainer def ("Tried to play sustainer SoundDef ... as a one-shot sound").
       CompAbilityEffect.Apply plays the comp's sound via PlayOneShot, so naming Core's def directly
       would give a red error and silence (decompile-verified 2026-07-25, RimWorld 1.6).

       Same grain folder, so this IS Core's throne-speech recording — the game's only human speech audio,
       and what vanilla plays when a pawn addresses a room (JobDriver_GiveSpeech, via soundDefMale /
       soundDefFemale on Core's gathering duty). That same job is where our speech bubble's symbol comes
       from, so the audio and the visual are drawn from one vanilla source.

       CORE-OWNED, not Royalty-gated, despite the clips' internal `Speech_Royal_*` names: the SoundDef
       carries no MayRequire, and the clips sit in the base game's resources.assets with zero hits in any
       of the five DLC asset bundles (verified 2026-07-25). So no MayRequire is needed here and no
       absence-fallback is required.

       What changed from Core, and why:
         • sustain True -> False, and the sustainer-only fields dropped (sustainLoop,
           sustainIntervalRange, and the pause/tempo fields that only matter to a maintained sound).
           Core's is a continuous murmur for a throne-room address running minutes; a rallying cry is one
           line at one moment. AudioGrain_Folder picks a single clip per play and each clip is a complete
           spoken phrase, so a one-shot is a whole utterance rather than a fragment of one.
         • volumeRange 8~10 -> 14~17. Core's is tuned to be half-heard across a throne room, which is too
           quiet for a deliberate shout, but this stays deliberately far below blast levels (cf.
           Explosion_Thump at 80): the clips are 8 kHz mono, chosen so speech reads as indistinct
           murmuring, and loud playback only exposes that. Landed at 14~17 by ear (user call,
           2026-07-25) after 20~24 — a touch under Core's own melee one-shots (Pawn_Melee_BigBash 20)
           and roughly 3 dB down from the first attempt.
       Clip lengths measured from the shipped AudioClip records: 0.72-2.09s across this male set of 22,
       against 1.50-4.92s for the female set of 21. So the cry's length varies with who holds the weapon
       — accepted as character (user call, 2026-07-25). Both sets fit inside the raised-weapon pose, so
       nothing is cut off. See RallyingCryShout_Female.xml for the other half of the pair. -->
  <SoundDef>
    <defName>UMW_RallyingCryShout_Male</defName>
    <context>MapOnly</context>
    <maxSimultaneous>1</maxSimultaneous>
    <subSounds>
      <li>
        <grains>
          <li Class="AudioGrain_Folder">
            <clipFolderPath>Misc/ThroneSpeech_Male</clipFolderPath>
          </li>
        </grains>
        <volumeRange>14~17</volumeRange>
        <distRange>20~40</distRange> <!-- Core's own falloff for this recording -->
      </li>
    </subSounds>
  </SoundDef>

</Defs>
